<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Exam problems on three-dimensional coordinates
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-3d-coordinates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-three-dimensional-coordinates" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the distance formula in space and describe regions bounded by spheres. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-three-dimensional-coordinates" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-3">
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find an equation of the set of all points equidistant from the points
<m>A(-1,5,3)</m> and <m>B(6,2,-2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the geometrical shape corresponding to the set.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We want to find all points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> that have the same distance
from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Squaring both distances (which is
equivalent, since distances are non-negative) gives
<md>
(x+1)^2 + (y-5)^2 + (z-3)^2 = (x-6)^2 + (y-2)^2 + (z+2)^2.
</md>
Expanding both sides,
<md>
<mrow>\amp x^2 + 2x + 1 + y^2 - 10y + 25 + z^2 - 6z + 9</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = x^2 - 12x + 36 + y^2 - 4y + 4 + z^2 + 4z + 4,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp 2x - 10y - 6z + 35 = -12x - 4y + 4z + 44,</mrow>
</md>
and collecting terms,
<md>
14x - 6y - 10z = 9.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
This is the equation of a plane. Its normal vector is
<m>\langle 14, -6, -10\rangle = 2\langle 7, -3, -5\rangle</m>, which
is parallel to
<m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \langle 6-(-1),\, 2-5,\, -2-3\rangle
= \langle 7, -3, -5\rangle</m>. Moreover, the midpoint of
<m>\overline{AB}</m>,
<md>
C\left(\frac{-1+6}{2},\, \frac{5+2}{2},\, \frac{3+(-2)}{2}\right)
= C\left(\frac52, \frac72, \frac12\right),
</md>
satisfies the equation, since
<m>14\left(\frac52\right) - 6\left(\frac72\right)
- 10\left(\frac12\right) = 35 - 21 - 5 = 9</m>. Hence the set is the
plane that passes through the midpoint of <m>\overline{AB}</m> and is
orthogonal to <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>, i.e. the <term>perpendicular bisector
plane</term> of the segment <m>\overline{AB}</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw22-bisector"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw22-bisector">
<caption>
The set of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is the plane
through the midpoint <m>C</m> of <m>\overline{AB}</m> perpendicular to
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
Two points A and B are joined by a segment. A shaded rectangle
representing a plane cuts the segment at right angles at its midpoint
C, with a small right angle marker at C.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % the plane, drawn as a slanted rectangle
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.25, rotate=-28] (-1.15,-1.9) rectangle (1.15,1.9);
  \draw[mred, thick, rotate=-28] (-1.15,-1.9) rectangle (1.15,1.9);
  % the segment AB
  \draw[thick] (-2.4,-0.6) -- (2.4,0.6);
  \fill (-2.4,-0.6) circle (2pt) node[left=2pt] {$A$};
  \fill (2.4,0.6) circle (2pt) node[right=2pt] {$B$};
  % the midpoint
  \fill[mblue] (0,0) circle (2pt);
  \node[mblue, above left=1pt] at (0,0.06) {$C$};
  % right-angle marker at C
  \draw[mred] (0.22,0.055) -- (0.28,-0.185) -- (0.06,-0.24);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-24">
<statement>
<p>
Describe the following regions and graphs.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4</m>, <m>z \ge 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4</m>, <m>z = 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4</m>, <m>z \ge 0</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4</m>, <m>z = 0</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The key is to notice whether the first condition is an
<em>inequality</em> (a solid region) or an <em>equation</em> (a
surface), and then what the second condition does to it.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The solid ball of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin, restricted
to <m>z \ge 0</m>: the <em>upper half of the solid ball</em>,
including the flat disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>z = 0</m> in the solid ball gives <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>:
the <em>solid disk</em> of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin,
lying in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The sphere of radius <m>2</m> restricted to <m>z \ge 0</m>: the
<em>upper hemisphere</em> (a surface only), together with the circle
where it meets the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>z = 0</m> on the sphere gives <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>: the
<em>circle</em> of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin in the
<m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m1-3">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the point <m>(3,7,-5)</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
What is its distance to the <m>yz</m>-plane?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is its distance to the <m>y</m>-axis?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The <m>yz</m>-plane is the plane <m>x = 0</m>. The
closest point of that plane to <m>(3,7,-5)</m> is obtained by simply
setting the <m>x</m>-coordinate to zero, giving <m>(0,7,-5)</m>. Hence
the distance is
<md>
\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (7-7)^2 + (-5+5)^2} = |3| = 3 .
</md>
In general the distance from <m>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> to the
<m>yz</m>-plane is <m>|x_0|</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The <m>y</m>-axis consists of the points
<m>(0,y,0)</m>. The closest such point to <m>(3,7,-5)</m> is the one
with the same <m>y</m>-coordinate, namely <m>(0,7,0)</m>, so the
distance is
<md>
\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (7-7)^2 + (-5-0)^2} = \sqrt{9+25} = \sqrt{34} .
</md>
In general the distance from <m>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> to the
<m>y</m>-axis is <m>\sqrt{x_0^2+z_0^2}</m>: we drop the coordinate
along the axis and keep the other two.
See <xref ref="fig-m1-distances"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m1-distances">
<caption>
The point <m>P(3,7,-5)</m>, its distance <m>3</m> to the
<m>yz</m>-plane (measured horizontally, parallel to the
<m>x</m>-axis), and its distance <m>\sqrt{34}</m> to the
<m>y</m>-axis (measured perpendicular to that axis).
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system with the point P at three, seven,
negative five. A segment runs from P horizontally to the point zero,
seven, negative five in the y z plane, and a second segment runs from P
to the point zero, seven, zero on the y axis.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (-1,0,0) -- (5,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1,0) -- (0,9,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-7) -- (0,0,3) node[above] {$z$};
% a patch of the yz-plane
\fill[mblue, opacity=0.14] (0,3.4,-6.8) -- (0,9.2,-6.8) -- (0,9.2,1.6)
      -- (0,3.4,1.6) -- cycle;
\node[mblue, anchor=south] at (0,6.3,1.7) {$yz$-plane};
% the point and its shadows
\coordinate (P) at (3,7,-5);
\coordinate (F) at (0,7,-5);
\coordinate (A) at (0,7,0);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (0,7,0) -- (F);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (3,0,0) -- (3,7,0) -- (P);
\draw[dashed, mgray] (3,7,0) -- (A);
\draw[very thick, mverm] (P) -- (F)
      node[midway, anchor=north west, inner sep=1pt] {$3$};
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (P) -- (A)
      node[midway, anchor=east] {$\sqrt{34}$\;};
\fill[mred] (P) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[mverm] (F) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[mgreen] (A) circle (1.6pt);
\node[mred, anchor=north east] at (P) {$P(3,7,-5)$\;};
\node[mverm, anchor=west] at (F) {\;$(0,7,-5)$};
\node[mgreen, anchor=south west] at (A) {\;$(0,7,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-describe-more">
<title>Describing More Equations and Inequalities Geometrically</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometrical meaning of the following equalities and
inequalities.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>y = -2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y = -2,\; z = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 + z^2 = 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 + z^2 \le 9,\; y \ge -1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y^2 + z^2 = 4,\; 0 \le x \le 5</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x = y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> This is the set of all points whose <m>y</m>-coordinate
equals <m>-2</m>, that is <m>\{(x,y,z) \in \R^3 : y = -2\}</m>. Both
<m>x</m> and <m>z</m> are unrestricted, so it is a plane parallel to the
<m>xz</m>-plane, sitting two units to the negative side of it, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-plane-y"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-plane-y">
<caption>
Part <alert>A</alert>: the plane <m>y=-2</m>, parallel to the
<m>xz</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded rectangle parallel to the x z plane, offset from it in the
negative y direction, representing the plane y equals negative two.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.75] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (-2.2,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.2,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-2.2);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\node[cyan!70!black] at (-1.8,-2,1.5) {$y=-2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>B.</alert> This is the intersection of the two planes
<m>y = -2</m> and <m>z = 1</m>, which is a line. Only <m>x</m> is
unrestricted, so the line is parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis and passes
through the point <m>(0,-2,1)</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-line-y-z"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-line-y-z">
<caption>
Part <alert>B</alert>: the planes <m>y=-2</m> and <m>z=1</m> meet in a
line parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis, highlighted in red.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
Two shaded planes, one for y equals negative two and one for z equals
one, crossing each other. Their intersection is a red line parallel to
the x axis.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.55] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\fill[orange!25, opacity=0.55] (-1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,2.2,1) -- (-1.8,2.2,1) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[orange!70!black] (-1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,2.2,1) -- (-1.8,2.2,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.2,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.2) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[red!70!black, very thick] (-1.8,-2,1) -- (1.8,-2,1);
\node[cyan!70!black, anchor=east] at (-1.9,-2,-1.85) {$y=-2$};
\node[orange!80!black, anchor=west] at (1.9,2.2,1) {$z=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>C.</alert> Comparing with the standard equation of a sphere, this
is the sphere of radius <m>3</m> centered at <m>(2,-1,0)</m>. Note the
center lies in the <m>xy</m>-plane, and since the radius is <m>3</m> the
sphere reaches from <m>z = -3</m> up to <m>z = 3</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-sphere-off"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-sphere-off">
<caption>
Part <alert>C</alert>: the sphere <m>(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2=9</m>, of
radius <m>3</m> centered at <m>(2,-1,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded sphere of radius three, offset from the origin toward the
point two, negative one, zero, with a dotted segment from the origin
to its center and a dashed ellipse marking its equator.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
\draw[gray, densely dotted] (0,0) -- (-2.78,-0.58);
\fill[gray] (-2.78,-0.58) circle (1pt);
\shade[ball color=cyan!30, opacity=0.92] (-2.78,-0.58) circle (1.55cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed, opacity=0.8] (-2.78,-0.58) ellipse (1.55cm and 0.43cm);
\node[gray!70!black, below=2pt] at (-2.78,-0.58) {$(2,-1,0)$};
% axes drawn last, so they stay visible where they cross the sphere;
% the x axis is shortened so its arrowhead and label sit in front of
% the sphere rather than buried in its middle
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.4,-0.9) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-0.69) node[below left=1pt] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0.05) {$0$};
\node[below] at (-2.78,-2.5) {$(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2=9$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>D.</alert> Replacing the equality by <m>\le</m> gives the solid
ball: every point whose distance from <m>(2,-1,0)</m> is at most
<m>3</m>. The extra condition <m>y \ge -1</m> keeps only the points on
one side of the plane <m>y = -1</m>. That plane passes through the
<em>center</em> of the ball, so exactly half of the ball survives: the
region is a solid half-ball of radius <m>3</m>, whose flat face is the
disk of radius <m>3</m> cut out of the plane <m>y = -1</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-halfball-off"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-halfball-off">
<caption>
Part <alert>D</alert>: the half-ball of radius <m>3</m> centered at
<m>(2,-1,0)</m>, cut by the condition <m>y \ge -1</m>, a plane through
its center.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded hemisphere sitting above a flat disk face at the plane y equals
negative one, offset from the origin toward the point two, negative one,
zero, with a dotted segment from the origin to the center of the disk.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
% the flat reference plane y=-1, exactly like part A's plane, so the
% orientation is guaranteed consistent
\fill[gray!15, opacity=0.7] (-2,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,3.7) -- (-2,-1,3.7) -- cycle;
\draw[gray!60, thin] (-2,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,3.7) -- (-2,-1,3.7) -- cycle;
% the curved dome (y from -1 up to the pole at y=2): stacked latitude
% circles of shrinking radius sqrt(9-(y+1)^2), each plotted directly in
% the true 3D coordinate system, giving a rounded, shaded cap
\foreach \y in {-0.85,-0.7,...,1.9} {
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\r}{sqrt(9-(\y+1)*(\y+1))}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\shd}{25+45*(\y+1)/3}
  \fill[cyan!\shd, opacity=0.85]
    plot[domain=0:360, samples=48, smooth cycle] ({2+\r*cos(\x)}, \y, {\r*sin(\x)});
}
% the flat cut disk (intersection with y=-1), same color family as the
% dome so the whole solid reads as one continuous surface
\fill[cyan!65, opacity=0.9]
  plot[domain=0:360, samples=72, smooth cycle] ({2+3*cos(\x)}, -1, {3*sin(\x)});
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick]
  plot[domain=0:360, samples=72, smooth cycle] ({2+3*cos(\x)}, -1, {3*sin(\x)});
% dotted line from the origin to the sphere's center
\draw[gray, densely dotted] (0,0,0) -- (2,-1,0);
\fill[gray] (2,-1,0) circle (1.3pt);
% axes drawn last, so they stay visible where they cross the shape
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (-2.2,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-3.9);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.9) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above right] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
\node[gray!60!black] at (-1.6,-1,3.6) {$y=-1$};
\node[gray!70!black, below right=1pt] at (2,-1,0) {$(2,-1,0)$};
\node[cyan!55!black, below] at (2,-1,-4.2) {$(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2 \le 9,\; y \ge -1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>E.</alert> In the <m>yz</m>-plane, <m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m> is a
circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin. In space <m>x</m> is
unrestricted, so the equation represents an infinitely long cylinder of
radius <m>2</m> whose axis is the <m>x</m>-axis. Compare this with
<xref ref="example-3d-describe"/>, where the missing variable was
<m>z</m> and the axis was therefore the <m>z</m>-axis: the axis of the
cylinder is always the axis of the variable that does not appear. The
left half of <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x"/> shows this cylinder.
</p>

<p>
<alert>F.</alert> The extra condition <m>0 \le x \le 5</m> cuts the
cylinder of part <alert>E</alert> down to a piece of length <m>5</m>,
running from the circle in the plane <m>x = 0</m> to the circle in the
plane <m>x = 5</m>, as on the right of
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x"/>. It is a tube, not a solid: the two
end disks are not included, since the equation forces
<m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m> exactly.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x">
<caption>
Parts <alert>E</alert> and <alert>F</alert>. On the left, the infinite
cylinder <m>y^2+z^2=4</m>, whose axis is the <m>x</m>-axis; the dashed
lines indicate that it continues without bound in both directions. On
the right, the same cylinder cut down to <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two pictures side by side. On the left, a tube of radius two lying along
the x axis, with dashed lines continuing past both ends to show that it
is unbounded. On the right, the same tube cut off by the plane x equals
zero at one end and the plane x equals five at the other, leaving a
piece of length five with open ends.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82, >=stealth]
% ------------- left: the infinite cylinder y^2+z^2=4 -------------
\begin{scope}
  % tube wall between the two drawn rims
  \fill[cyan!22, opacity=0.8] (-1.4,-1.5) rectangle (3.4,1.5);
  % both rim disks lighter than the wall: the tube is open, not capped,
  % so only the side wall gets the solid body color
  \fill[cyan!10, opacity=0.9] (-1.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \fill[cyan!10, opacity=0.9] (3.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-1.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (3.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.4,1.5) -- (3.4,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.4,-1.5) -- (3.4,-1.5);
  % dashed continuations: the cylinder is unbounded in both directions
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.4,1.5) -- (-2.1,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.4,-1.5) -- (-2.1,-1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (3.4,1.5) -- (4.1,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (3.4,-1.5) -- (4.1,-1.5);
  % axes last, so they stay visible where they pass through the tube
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-1.05) node[below left] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \node[below] at (1.0,-2.2) {$y^2+z^2=4$};
\end{scope}
% ------------- right: the piece with 0 <= x <= 5 -------------
\begin{scope}[xshift=8.2cm]
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-1.05) node[below left] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (-1.3,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \fill[purple!20, opacity=0.8] (0,-1.5) rectangle (3.6,1.5);
  % both rim disks lighter than the wall: the tube is open, not capped,
  % so only the side wall gets the solid body color
  \fill[purple!8, opacity=0.9] (0,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \fill[purple!8, opacity=0.9] (3.6,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (0,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (3.6,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (0,1.5) -- (3.6,1.5);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (0,-1.5) -- (3.6,-1.5);
  \node[purple!60!black, above] at (0,1.6) {$x=0$};
  \node[purple!60!black, above] at (3.6,1.6) {$x=5$};
  \node[below] at (1.8,-2.2) {$y^2+z^2=4,\; 0\le x\le 5$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>G.</alert> The condition <m>x = y</m> says nothing about
<m>z</m>, so whenever a point satisfies it, so does the whole vertical
line through that point. In the <m>xy</m>-plane the equation
<m>x = y</m> describes a line through the origin, and sweeping that line
vertically gives a plane: the plane containing the <m>z</m>-axis that
cuts the <m>xy</m>-plane along the line <m>y = x</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-plane-xy"/>. It is <em>not</em> parallel to any
coordinate plane, unlike part <alert>A</alert>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-plane-xy">
<caption>
Part <alert>G</alert>: the plane <m>x = y</m>. It contains the
<m>z</m>-axis and meets the <m>xy</m>-plane in the line <m>y = x</m>,
drawn in red.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded plane standing vertically and cutting diagonally through the
first and third octants. It contains the z axis, and the red line where
it meets the horizontal x y plane runs diagonally between the x and y
axes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.95, >=stealth]
% the plane x = y is spanned by the directions (1,1,0) and (0,0,1)
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.7]
  (-2.0,-2.0,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,1.9) -- (-2.0,-2.0,1.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black]
  (-2.0,-2.0,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,1.9) -- (-2.0,-2.0,1.9) -- cycle;
% axes, negative parts drawn thin so the plane reads as passing through the origin
\draw[thick] (-1.8,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-1.4,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.2,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-1.7) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.4) node[above] {$z$};
% the trace in the xy-plane
\draw[red!70!black, very thick] (-2.0,-2.0,0) -- (3.6,3.6,0);
\node[red!70!black, below right=1pt] at (3.6,3.6,0) {$y=x$};
\node[cyan!70!black] at (0.9,0.9,1.55) {$x=y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-cylinder-plane-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Cylinder and a Plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid region bounded by the three surfaces
<md>
z = 0, \qquad x^2+y^2 = 4, \qquad x+y+z = 4,
</md>
restricted to <m>z \ge 0</m>. Describe each of the three bounding
surfaces, and describe the solid region they enclose.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The equation <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> is missing <m>z</m>, so it is the
infinite cylinder of radius <m>2</m> whose axis is the <m>z</m>-axis.
The equation <m>z=0</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane, and <m>x+y+z=4</m> is a
plane tilted with respect to all three coordinate axes.
</p>
<p>
Solving the third equation for <m>z</m> gives <m>z = 4-x-y</m>. Over
the disk <m>x^2+y^2 \le 4</m> enclosed by the cylinder, this height
stays positive: the largest value of <m>x+y</m> on that disk is
<m>2\sqrt2 \approx 2.83</m>, attained in the direction <m>(1,1)</m>, so
<m>z = 4-x-y \ge 4-2\sqrt2 &gt; 0</m> throughout the disk. The tilted
plane therefore never dips below the <m>xy</m>-plane over the region
enclosed by the cylinder.
</p>
<p>
The solid is the piece of the solid cylinder <m>x^2+y^2 \le 4</m>
lying between the flat base <m>z=0</m> and the slanted lid
<m>z=4-x-y</m>: an oblique, truncated cylinder whose base is the disk
of radius <m>2</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and whose slanted top is an
ellipse, shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-plane-solid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-plane-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded by <m>z=0</m>, the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, and the
tilted plane <m>x+y+z=4</m>. The disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the
flat base, and the plane, hatched in pink, cuts the cylinder in an
ellipse that forms the slanted top.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A cylinder of radius two centered on the z axis, standing on a
shaded disk in the x y plane. The cylinder is sliced off at a slant by
a tilted plane, hatched in pink, that meets it in an ellipse forming
the top of the solid.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.65]
\tikzmath{function a(\x,\y) {return (4.0-\x-\y);};}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\ejez}{a(0,0)}
% the equation of the circumference
\draw[white] (0,-2.5,0) -- (2.75,0,0) node[blue,midway,below,sloped] {\footnotesize$x^2 + y^2 = 4$};
% the base disk of radius 2
\draw[thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (3.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-3,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-3,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,\ejez);
% the plane x+y+z=4
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Az}{a(2.5,2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Bz}{a(-2.5,2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Cz}{a(-2.5,-2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Dz}{a(2.5,-2.5)}
\coordinate (A) at (2.5,2.5,\Az);
\coordinate (B) at (-2.5,2.5,\Bz);
\coordinate (C) at (-2.5,-2.5,\Cz);
\coordinate (D) at (2.5,-2.5,\Dz);
% the cylinder's side wall, drawn as vertical segments up to the plane
\foreach \angulo in {0,0.01,...,\final}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,opacity=0.5] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0-\px-\py);
}
% the intersection of the cylinder and the plane (the slanted top)
\draw[blue,thick] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2.0*sin(\t r)-2.0*cos(\t r)});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2831853,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2*sin(\t r)-2.0*cos(\t r)});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2831853,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% the plane, hatched
\draw[white] (C) -- (B) node[red,above,sloped,midway]{\footnotesize$x + y + z = 4$};
\draw[red,dash dot] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pattern color=pink,pattern=north east lines] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pink,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2.0*sin(\t r) - 2.0*cos(\t r)});
% z axis, drawn last so it stays visible above the solid
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,\ejez) -- (0,0,8) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<title>Graphing a Parabolic Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the graph of <m>z = 4 - x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
Why is this surface called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Since the equation <m>z=4-x^2</m> does not involve <m>y</m>, every
cross-section of the surface at a fixed value of <m>y</m> is the
<em>same</em> parabola <m>z=4-x^2</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, opening
downward from its vertex at <m>(0,4)</m>. Sliding that parabola along
the <m>y</m>-axis from <m>y=0</m> to <m>y=4</m> sweeps out the surface.
</p>
<p>
Over <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, the parabola satisfies <m>4-x^2 \ge 0</m>,
with equality exactly at <m>x=\pm 2</m>, so the surface touches the
<m>xy</m>-plane along the two edges <m>x=\pm2</m> of the rectangular
region and rises to a maximum height of <m>4</m> along the ridge
<m>x=0</m>. Because it is generated by sliding a fixed curve along a
straight line, rather than curving in the <m>y</m> direction as well,
it is called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<caption>
The parabolic cylinder <m>z=4-x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>. Every slice at a fixed
<m>y</m> is the same downward-opening parabola.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A curved sheet shaped like a downward opening parabola in the x z
plane, extended without change along the y direction over a rectangular
region in the x y plane, forming an arched, tunnel-like surface.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.85]
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (2.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-0.5,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0.0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the rectangular region in the xy-plane
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
% the surface
\foreach \y in {0,\step,...,4.0}{
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{\y},{4.0 - \t*\t});
}
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{4.0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
\node[blue,above right] at (0,2.5,4.125) {$z = 4 - x^2$};
% last part of the z axis
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-nonconvex-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid, a Plane, and a Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid region bounded below by the paraboloid
<m>z = 1-x^2-y^2</m>, above by the plane <m>z=1</m>, and on the outside
by the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. Describe this solid.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The paraboloid <m>z=1-x^2-y^2</m> opens downward from its vertex
<m>(0,0,1)</m>, reaching <m>z=0</m> exactly on the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, the same circle where the cylinder meets the
<m>xy</m>-plane. The plane <m>z=1</m> is tangent to the paraboloid at
its vertex.
</p>
<p>
The solid consists of the points inside the cylinder that lie
<em>above</em> the paraboloid and <em>below</em> the plane:
<md>
x^2+y^2 \le 1, \qquad 1-x^2-y^2 \le z \le 1.
</md>
At the center <m>(x,y)=(0,0)</m>, this range for <m>z</m> collapses to
the single point <m>z=1</m>, since the paraboloid already reaches the
plane there. At the rim <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, the range is the full
interval <m>0 \le z \le 1</m>. The solid is therefore a disk-shaped
shell that thins to a single point at its center, as though a
dome-shaped cavity bounded by the paraboloid had been hollowed out from
the top of the solid cylinder <m>x^2+y^2\le1</m>, <m>0\le z\le 1</m>,
shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-nonconvex-solid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-nonconvex-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded below by the paraboloid <m>z=1-x^2-y^2</m>, above by
the plane <m>z=1</m>, and on the outside by the cylinder
<m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. The paraboloid hollows out a dome-shaped cavity from
the top of the solid, which is thin at the center and full height at
the rim.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<description>
<p>
A short solid cylinder whose flat top has a dome-shaped dent scooped
out of it. The dent is bounded by a downward-curving paraboloid that
touches the flat top at the center and meets the bottom rim of the
cylinder at its edge, leaving a thin shell of material near the rim and
almost none at the center.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=2.0]
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tini}{0.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tfin}{1.85*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tend}{2.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
\coordinate (A) at (1.25,1.25,1);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.25,1.25,1);
\coordinate (C) at (-1.25,-1.5,1);
\coordinate (D) at (1.25,-1.5,1);
% node indicating the equation of the circumference
\draw[white] (1.35,0,0) -- (0,1.35,0) node [black,below,midway,sloped] {$x^2 + y^2 = 1$};
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (1.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-1.25,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,1.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-1.25,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1.0);
% the base disk
\fill[yellow,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[gray,thick] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[black,thick,opacity=0.75] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% the curves slicing the paraboloid
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-1:1,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},0,{1.0 - \t*\t});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-1:1,smooth,variable=\t] (0,{\t},{1.0 - \t*\t});
\foreach \angulo in {0,2,...,358}{
  \draw[cyan,very thick,rotate around z=\angulo,opacity=0.15] plot[domain=0:1,smooth,variable=\t] ({0},{\t},{1.0 - \t*\t});
}
% the paraboloid (drawn as circles of constant height)
\foreach \altura in {0.0125,0.025,...,1.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=\tini:\tfin,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{1.0 - \altura});
}
% first part of the z axis
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,1.0) -- (0,0,1.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\foreach \altura in {0.0125,0.025,...,1.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=\tfin:\tend,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{1.0 - \altura});
}
\node[blue,right] at (0,0.5,1.125) {$z = 1 - x^2 - y^2$};
% the outer cylinder wall
\foreach \angulo in {0,0.01,...,\final}{
  \pgfmathparse{cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[gray,opacity=0.5] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,1.0);
}
% the circle at z = 1
\draw[black,thick,opacity=0.75] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({cos(\t r)},{sin(\t r)},{1.0});
% the plane z = 1
\draw[white] (C) -- (B) node[red,above,sloped,midway]{\footnotesize$z = 1$};
\draw[red,dash dot] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pattern color=pink,pattern=north east lines] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,1.0) -- (0,0,1.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Cylinder and a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid bounded by the three surfaces
<md>
z = 0, \qquad z = x^2+y^2, \qquad x^2+y^2 = 4.
</md>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Describe each of the three bounding surfaces.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the solid region they enclose.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The equation <m>z=0</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane. The
equation <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> is a paraboloid opening upward from its
vertex at the origin. The equation <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> is missing
<m>z</m>, so it is the infinite cylinder of radius <m>2</m> whose axis
is the <m>z</m>-axis. The paraboloid meets that cylinder exactly where
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, at height <m>z=4</m>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(b)</term> The solid consists of the points inside the cylinder
lying between the flat base and the paraboloid:
<md>
x^2+y^2 \le 4, \qquad 0 \le z \le x^2+y^2.
</md>
Near the <m>z</m>-axis this range for <m>z</m> is almost nothing, since
<m>x^2+y^2 \approx 0</m> there, while at the rim <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> it is
the full interval <m>0 \le z \le 4</m>. The solid is therefore shaped
like a shallow bowl carved out from underneath the paraboloid: thin at
the center and rising to its full height <m>4</m> at the cylinder wall,
shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid"/>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(c)</term> Setting <m>y=0</m> restricts the solid's defining
inequalities to <m>-2\le x\le 2</m> and <m>0\le z\le x^2</m>: the
region under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m>, above the <m>x</m>-axis,
between <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section">
<caption>
The cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>: the region
under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m> for <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="40%">
<description>
<p>
A two dimensional graph in the x z plane. A shaded region lies under
the upward opening parabola z equals x squared, above the x axis,
between x equals negative two and x equals two.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% the region 0 <= z <= x^2 for -2 <= x <= 2
\fill[cyan!35, opacity=0.85] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
\draw[->, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$z$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[dashed, gray] (2,0) -- (2,4);
\draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,4);
\node[below] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[cyan!55!black, above right] at (1.3,2.0) {$z=x^2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded by <m>z=0</m>, the paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m>, and
the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>. The solid fills the region under the
paraboloid, thin near the <m>z</m>-axis and reaching full height where
the paraboloid meets the cylinder wall.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A paraboloid opening upward from the origin, shown as a shaded bowl
sitting inside a cylindrical wall of radius two. The bowl's rim meets
the top of the cylinder, and a shaded disk in the x y plane forms the
flat base of the solid beneath the bowl.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{130}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.75]
\tikzmath{function f(\x) {return \x;};}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zini}{0.5*sqrt(2.0)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zsig}{\zini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\nextz}{\zini+0.5*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\sig}{2.0*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tini}{0.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tnext}{\tini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tfin}{1.85*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tend}{2.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tNext}{\tfin+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (3.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-2.5,0);
% the base disk
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (-2,0,0) -- (-2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,-2,0) -- (0,-2,4);
% the cylinder around the paraboloid (back)
\foreach \angulo in {\tini,\tnext,...,\tfin}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tini:\tfin,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the curves bounding the solid
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},0,{\t*\t});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] (0,{\t},{\t*\t});
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tfin:\tend,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0});
% the cylinder out of the paraboloid (front)
\foreach \angulo in {\tfin,\tNext,...,\tend}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (2,0,0) -- (2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,2,0) -- (0,2,4);
\node[blue,left] at (1,-1.25,2.5) {$z = x^2 + y^2$};
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

